# /{{SLUG}} - talk to {{NAME}}

Global MindAttic command (MAT-T89), one per org member. The user typed it, so it is their explicit
ask to reach {{NAME}} ({{EMAIL}}).

`$ARGUMENTS` starts with a verb or it doesn't:

- **`research <what>`**: follow `~/.claude/commands/research.md` exactly, with `<who>` = `{{EMAIL}}`
  and `<what>` = everything after the word `research`.
- **`pair [repo]`**: you and {{NAME}} work one repo side by side, each on your own share of its open
  tickets, never both on the same one (MAT-T104). `<Repo>` is the repo named, else the one the current
  directory is in. `<you>` is this CLI's own login email (the account the session's [Billing] line
  names).
  1. Split the work: `tickets ticket divvy --repo <Repo> --member <you> --member {{EMAIL}}`. It assigns
     every unassigned open ticket to one of you (balanced by estimate, prerequisite chains kept with
     one person, tickets already being worked left alone), and the Orchestrator then dispatches each
     only on its assignee's login. Keep its printout for step 5.
  2. Call ListAgents. Its first line names this session ("This session is <name> [<ref>]"): that is
     the address {{NAME}}'s CLI reports to.
  3. Write this brief to a scratchpad file, filled in: "Pairing with <you> on <Repo>, from the session
     <name> [<ref>]. The user asked for this pair session, so start /allocate -repo=<Repo> now through
     the Skill tool: your share is the <Repo> tickets assigned to {{EMAIL}}, and /allocate hands you
     only those (or unassigned ones); <you>'s share goes only to <you>'s login. Working together: put
     anything the other side should know about a ticket in `tickets ticket note <ID> "<markdown>"`;
     when a ticket of yours needs one of theirs first, link it (`tickets ticket block <ID> --by <THEIRS>`)
     and SendMessage <name> naming both. When /allocate says your share has run dry, run
     `tickets ticket divvy --repo <Repo> --member <you> --member {{EMAIL}} --rebalance` (it moves only
     unstarted tickets, just enough to even the shares) and carry on; when it moves nothing and
     nothing of yours is left, SendMessage <name>: 'pair <Repo>: {{EMAIL}}'s share is done'."
     If a running peer session is plainly {{NAME}}'s in <Repo>, SendMessage it that text; otherwise run
     `$p = (Get-Content -Raw <file>).Replace('"', "'"); orchestrator org start <Repo> {{EMAIL}} --prompt $p`.
  4. Start your own share here: invoke /allocate -repo=<Repo> through the Skill tool (the user's
     `pair` asked for it), and follow the same working-together rules as the brief, with `<you>` and
     {{EMAIL}} swapped - rebalance the same way when yours runs dry.
  5. Tell the user in a few lines: how many tickets each of you got (divvy's share lines), what it
     left alone, and that {{NAME}}'s CLI was started or messaged. Proposed (unapproved) tickets are
     split too but /allocate won't start them until approved (`tickets ticket approve`); say how many
     of each share wait for that. {{NAME}}'s messages arrive here as notifications; don't poll.
- **Anything else is a message to {{NAME}}**, verbatim:
  1. Call ListAgents. Its first line names this session ("This session is <name> [<ref>]"): that is
     the reply address.
  2. If a running peer session is plainly {{NAME}}'s (its row names their login, {{EMAIL}}, or a
     session they started for this), SendMessage it: "From <your login's email> (reply to <name>):
     <message>". If two match, send to the one in this repo.
  3. Otherwise start one on their login in this repo: write the text "Message from <your login's
     email>, sent from the session <name> [<ref>]: <message>. Answer it, and send your reply back
     with SendMessage to <name>." to a scratchpad file, then run
     `$p = (Get-Content -Raw <file>).Replace('"', "'"); orchestrator org start <Repo> {{EMAIL}} --prompt $p`
     (the repo is the one the current directory is in; MindAttic.Agency in the workspace root).
  4. Tell the user in one line where it went. Their reply arrives here as a notification. Don't wait
     or poll for it.
